#
# The Kconfig symbols every wz Zephyr application shares. An application's own
# `Kconfig` sources this file, then Zephyr's (`source "Kconfig.zephyr"`).

menu "wz Zephyr profile"

config WZ_ENABLE_CORE_CACHES
	bool "Enable the core's instruction and data caches at boot"
	depends on CACHE_MANAGEMENT
	help
	  Zephyr's port of some SoCs never enables the Cortex-M7's caches although
	  CONFIG_ICACHE and CONFIG_DCACHE are set (the Infineon CAT1C port is one: read
	  from a running T2G node, SCB->CCR had both cache bits clear, and the core ran
	  every instruction from flash without a cache). Select this on a board whose
	  port is such a one.

	  The instruction cache is enabled. The data cache is enabled only when the
	  build has a non-cacheable section (CONFIG_NOCACHE_MEMORY) and the MPU marks
	  every line of it non-cacheable, which the image reads from the MPU's own
	  registers at boot, after the MPU is set up: the MAC's descriptor rings and
	  buffer pools live in that section, and a bus master reading or writing
	  cacheable memory corrupts frames without any other symptom. When that does
	  not hold the data cache stays off and the console says why, in the line
	  `wz: caches: ...`.

config WZ_ENTROPY_BOARD_HOOK
	bool "The board supplies its own entropy source"
	help
	  The session draws its cookie nonces and its cookie-signing key through
	  `wzApplicationGetRandom`, which deploy/zephyr-common/wz_board_hooks.c answers
	  from Zephyr's entropy device when the build has one.

	  Select this on a board whose true random number generator is not a Zephyr
	  entropy driver: the board's own source file then defines
	  `int wz_board_entropy_fill(void *buf, size_t len)`, which fills the buffer
	  from the hardware TRNG and returns 0, or a non-zero value when it cannot. It
	  must fail rather than return what the buffer held.

	  Never a way to silence the build on a board that has no source: a board
	  with neither this nor an entropy driver does not compile, by design.

config WZ_CORE_CLOCK_BOARD_HOOK
	bool "The board reads its core clock from the chip's registers"
	help
	  An image is built for one core clock (CONFIG_SYS_CLOCK_HW_CYCLES_PER_SEC) and
	  nothing in the kernel checks that the silicon runs at it. On a chip whose
	  clock tree another core must bring up, a core that was started without that
	  runs at the oscillator and every wait lasts many times what it was asked to.

	  Select this on a board that can read the clock it really runs at: the board's
	  own source file then defines `uint32_t wz_board_core_clock_hz(void)`, which
	  returns that clock in hertz from the registers, or 0 when it cannot tell. The
	  image compares it with the clock it assumes before it waits for anything.

	  A board without this answers 0 to the image and is not checked, which is what
	  every emulated board does.

config WZ_LIBC_MEMCPY_FIRST
	bool "Link the C library before the Rust staticlib, so its memcpy family is the image's"
	help
	  The Rust staticlib carries `compiler_builtins`, whose generic, weak `memcpy`, `memmove`,
	  `memset` and `memcmp` are the first definitions the linker meets when the C library is
	  scanned after it (Zephyr puts it last), so the whole image (the kernel, the network
	  stack, every C application beside the Rust) runs them and never the library's own, which
	  on a Cortex-M is assembly. With this on, the library is scanned first and its strong
	  definitions are the image's.

	  MEASURED on the T2G kit (Cortex-M7, 350 MHz), same MAC, driver and Zephyr stack, three
	  rounds of 800 GETs, images alternated: with CONFIG_SPEED_OPTIMIZATIONS=y a GET answered by
	  the wz node takes 0.717 ms without this and 0.689 with it (the node's own turnaround on
	  the wire 0.478 -> 0.420 ms), and one answered by zenoh-pico 0.406 -> 0.380 ms. With the
	  default size optimization it is the other way: 0.805 -> 0.855 ms. So it is off by default
	  and an image that is built for speed turns it on; an image whose grade rests on a
	  witnessed run keeps the bytes it was witnessed with.

endmenu
